  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: Generally quiet conditions are anticipated over\nthe next couple days, with minor caveats. A Southern stream\nshortwave extending from Texas into the Deep South is yielding \nmid and high clouds across the area early this morning. As it \nshifts eastward overnight tonight, scattered sprinkles and light \nshowers will be possible tomorrow morning, mainly across south \nMS. Because amounts are not expected to be measurable in most \nareas, we aren't currently advertising PoPs, however, low end \nchances may need to be added to the forecast if high res guidance\ntrends wetter. Then, as a northern stream upper trough begins to \ndeepen across the eastern part of the country tomorrow afternoon, \nit will send a cold front through the Mid South and into our area \ntomorrow night. Isolated showers are also possible ahead of this \nfront, mainly across north and east MS. Again with this front, \nany rain amounts will be light, and development will struggle \nagainst dry air in the lower levels. Overall, most locations in \nour area will stay dry through this time frame, with temperatures\ncontinuing to trend milder.\n\nThursday through Monday: Dry weather conditions will continue in \nthe wake of the cold front. The pressure gradient will increase \nThursday, resulting in breezy conditions throughout much of the \nday. Given the ongoing drought conditions and anticipated low\ndaytime humidity, the addition of wind gusts to the equation will\nincrease fire danger potential on Thursday. This could carry over\ninto Friday, but wind should be subsiding by then, and decreasing\ntemps will become less favorable for fire growth. For now, we \nhave opted to highlight a limited fire threat for Thursday to \nbring awareness to this.\n\nHeading into the weekend, there remains solid potential for \nsubfreezing temps Friday night and Saturday night over at least a\nportion of the area. As mentioned in previous discussions, our \narea will be on the western edge of the core of this colder \nairmass, meaning the chances for a freeze are higher in the \nGolden Triangle and lower in our CenLa and MissLou areas. As we \nget closer in time to the weekend, we will highlight the areas \nmost likely to be impacted in HWO graphics.\n\nFinally at the end of the forecast period, a secondary trough may\nbring rain showers late Sunday into Monday, but guidance is far \nfrom harmonious on this potential and chances are very low for the\ntime being. /DL/",
